.TH OPENTALK-COMPOSE 1 "February 2025" "Version 1.0" "EXPERIMENTAL User Manual"

.SH NAME
opentalk-compose \- Compose and orchestrate OpenTalk services.

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cd /opt/opentalk-compose 
.RS
IMPORTANT: all commands needs to be executed from /opt/opentalk-compose
.RE

.B cforge init
.RS
Runs the cforge container, which initializes the configuration of services. It must be run at least once before the services can be started.
After initialization use docker compose to run the applications stack.
.RE

.B cforge apply
.RS
Apply changes in settings.yml to the service configurations. Changes also provoke a restart of a relatet service. Currently it does not include a recreation of a container.
Changes on the compose override files needs still a `docker compose up -d` to get applied.
.RE


.B cforge task <task name>
.RS
Executes a post-installation task.
.RE

.RS
Example:
.EX
.B cforge task demoUser
.EE
.RE

.B docker compose up -d
.RS
Starts the entire OpenTalk stack in the background.
.RE

.B docker compose restart <service name>
.RS
Restarts a single OpenTalk service.
.RE

.RS
Example:
.EX
docker compose restart controller
.EE
.RE

.SH DESCRIPTION
The package opentalk-compose contains compose.yaml files to run a complete OpenTalk instance on a single node.
It is primarily intended to be used with Docker Compose to orchestrate the services and configuration.
You must ensure that the Docker container engine and the Docker Compose plugin are properly installed and functioning.

The cforge container is specifically responsible for configuration management. Manual configuration is not recommended, as the application stack consists of many interdependent services.

All commands must be executed from the directory /opt/opentalk-compose.

.SH INSTALLATION
After installing the package opentalk-compose, the installation can be continued in three phases: preparation, initialization, and optionally post-install. Follow the instructions below to successfully complete the installation of OpenTalk.

.SS Preparation:
It must be ensured that the following preparations have been made befor the initialization can run.

.B 1. The host has a valid domain name record pointing to the public IP address.
.RS
Running `getent hosts opentalk.example.com` on your server must show the public IP address.
.RE

.B 2. The following ports are used by default and must be accessible on the public interface if the respective services are utilized:
.RS
* 80,443/tcp (HTTP, HTTPS)
.RE
.RS
* 40000-49999/udp (RTP Obelisk)
.RE
.RS
* 5060/udp (SIP Obelisk)
.RE
.RS
* 50000-64535/udp (RTP LiveKit)
.RE
.RS
* 7881/tcp (WebRTC ICE over TCP, LiveKit)
.RE

.SS Initialization:
During initialization, cforge generates configuration files for the services to be started. Therefore, it is crucial that the domain
is correctly set as the value for 'OT_DOMAIN' in config/default.env. Secrets for the individual services are also generated and stored in config/secrets/ and in
the corresponding configuration files. 

.B 1. Change to the directory /opt/opentalk-compose
.RS
cd /opt/opentalk-compose
.RE

.B 2. Add the domain name record (e.g., opentalk.example.com) to the variable 'OT_DOMAIN' in the configuration file config/default.env
.RS
sed -i 's/^OT_DOMAIN=.*/OT_DOMAIN="opentalk.example.com"/' /opt/opentalk-compose/config/default.env
.RE

.B 3. Run the initialization using cforge
.RS
cforge init
.RE

.B 4. Start the OpenTalk application stack
.RS
docker compose up -d
.RE

.SS Post-Install:
Some configurations or tasks can only be performed once certain services have successfully started.
These are handled as post-install tasks and can only be executed after the initialization has
been successfully completed and services have started.

.B Post-Install Tasks can be executed as follows:
.RS
cforge task <task name>
.RE

.B Currently available post-install tasks that can be set as TAGS:
.RS
.EX

+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+
| Task Name                  | Task Description                                       |
+----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------+
| demoUser                   | Creates a user in Keycloak for testing purposes.       |
| outlookClient              | Creates a OIDC client for the OpenTalk Outlook Add-in. |
| selfSignedCerts            | Creates self-signed certificates for the OpenTalk.     |
|                            |                                                        |
| createBackup               | Creates full backup of all services                    |
|   createBackupConfig       | Creates backup of the config directory                 |
|   createBackupControllerDB | Creates backup of the Controller database              |
|   createBackupKeycloakDB   | Creates backup of the Keycloak database                |
|   createBackupTraefik      | Creates backup of the Traefik data directory           |
|   createBackupMinio        | Creates backup of MinIO bucket                         |
|                            |                                                        |
| restoreBackup              | Restore OpenTalk from a given timestamp. A full backup |
|                            |   archive is required                                  |
|   restoreBackupConfig      | Restores the config directory from a given timestamp   |
|   restoreBackupControllerDB| Restores Controller database from a given timestamp    |
|   restoreBackupKeycloakDB  | Restores Keycloak database from a given timestamp      |
|   restoreBackupTraefik     | Restores Traefik data directory from a given timestamp |
|   restoreBackupMinio       | Restores MinIO bucket from a given timestamp           |
+----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+
.EE
.RE

.SH BACKUPS:
Backups can be created as post-install tasks describes above. The backup task bundels all files
as '.tgz' archive with the current timestamp in the directory '/var/opt/opentalk-compose/backups'.
When you run a single node setup, the 'restoreBackup' task is recommended.

.RS
Example:
.EX
cforge task createBackup
.EE
.RE



.SH RESTORE:
A Restore is also performed as post-install task. But the variable BACKUP_TIMESTAMP defined with
the timestamp of the backup archive to restore is required. The backup archive will be extracted
in /var/opt/opentalk-compose/backups/tmp/<timestamp> and the files will synced back and database 
dumps will be improrted.

.B IMPORTANT!
A restore will overwrite existing data!

.SS Scenario 1 - Restore a running OpenTalk instance
Your OpenTalk installation is functioning but you want to rollback to an earlier release or restore
deleted files.

.RS
Example:
.EX
cforge task restoreBackup --timestamp 20250207144600Z
docker compose up -d --force-recreate
.EE
.RE

.SS Scenario 2 - Desaster recovery or move to a new server
You are running an new machine or a fresh environment and your OpenTalk installation is not
initialized. To Restore in this Scenario, you need to sync back the configuration files first
using the task 'restoreBackupConfig'. After that initialze OpenTalk and bring it into a
functioning state. When OpenTalk is running, you can import databases and other persistant data.

.RS
Example:
.EX
cforge task restoreBackupConfig --timestamp 20250207144600Z
cforge init
docker compose up -d
.EE
.RE

